  "notes": [
    "Tea Style & Origin: Phoenix oolong (traditionally known as *Fenghuang Dancong*) originates from the Phoenix Mountain region of Guangdong, China, and is crafted as long, twisted strip-style leaves celebrated for naturally occurring stone-fruit and floral aromatics.",
    "Western Brewing Method: Use 1 teaspoon (about 2–3 grams) of leaves per 8 oz of water heated to 195°F–205°F (just under boiling), steeping for 2 to 3 minutes.",
    "Traditional Gongfu Brewing: For concentrated aroma and sweetness, brew in a small teapot or gaiwan using a higher leaf ratio (5–7 grams per 100–120 ml) with near-boiling water and quick steeps starting at 10 to 15 seconds.",
    "Multiple Infusions: Whole-leaf Phoenix oolong is designed for re-steeping; each portion of leaves can be infused several times, with new aromatic layers revealing themselves in each round.",
    "Package Yield: A 0.5 oz (approximately 14 g) sample pouch yields roughly 5 to 7 standard 8 oz mugs brewed Western-style, or 2 to 3 multi-steep Gongfu sessions."
  ],
